SELECT '860703c1-1675-7df0-a463-73dc93baa06c', 'be88f6ab-5f8b-7754-8653-d472b7389fec', 'Two Sum', 'Two Sum', 'Difficulty: Easy · LeetCode 1
Problem
Given an array of integers nums and an integer target, return indices of the two numbers that add up to target. Exactly one solution exists. You may not use the same element twice.
Examples
Input
nums: int[], target: int
2 ≤ n ≤ 104
Output
int[2] — indices [i, j] where nums[i]+nums[j]=target
Example 1
nums=[2,7,11,15], target=9
Output: [0,1]
// 2+7=9
Example 2
nums=[3,2,4], target=6
Output: [1,2]
Constraints
2 ≤ nums.length ≤ 104
-109 ≤ nums[i], target ≤ 109
Only one valid answer exists
Understanding the problem
For each element x, we need target - x. Brute force checks all pairs O(n²). Optimization: store each element in a map as we scan, look up the complement in O(1).
Intuition
For each element x, we need target - x. Store visited elements in {value: index} map. Check if the complement exists before inserting current element.
Approach
Init empty map seen = {value: index}.
For each index i: compute complement = target - nums[i].
If complement in seen → return [seen[complement], i].
Else store seen[nums[i]] = i.
Naive Solution — O(n²)
Check all pairs (i, j) with i < j.
Expected Output
[2,7,11,15],target=9 → [0,1]
class Solution:
    def twoSum(self, nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:
        for i in range(len(nums)):
            for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):
                if nums[i] + nums[j] == target:
                    return [i, j]
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++)
            for (int j = i+1; j < nums.size(); j++)
                if (nums[i]+nums[j]==target) return {i,j};
        return {};
    }
};
class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++)
            for (int j = i+1; j < nums.length; j++)
                if (nums[i]+nums[j]==target) return new int[]{i,j};
        return new int[]{};
    }
}
var twoSum = function(nums, target) {
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++)
        for (let j = i+1; j < nums.length; j++)
            if (nums[i]+nums[j]===target) return [i,j];
};
Time: O(n²) · Space: O(1)
Optimized Code — One-pass HashMap
Expected Output
[2,7,11,15],9 → [0,1] | [3,2,4],6 → [1,2] | [3,3],6 → [0,1]
class Solution:
    def twoSum(self, nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:
        seen = {}
        for i, num in enumerate(nums):
            complement = target - num
            if complement in seen:
                return [seen[complement], i]
            seen[num] = i
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        unordered_map<int,int> seen;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            int comp = target - nums[i];
            if (seen.count(comp)) return {seen[comp], i};
            seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }
        return {};
    }
};
class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        Map<Integer,Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int comp = target - nums[i];
            if (seen.containsKey(comp)) return new int[]{seen.get(comp),i};
            seen.put(nums[i], i);
        }
        return new int[]{};
    }
}
var twoSum = function(nums, target) {
    const seen = new Map();
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        const comp = target - nums[i];
        if (seen.has(comp)) return [seen.get(comp), i];
        seen.set(nums[i], i);
    }
};
Time: O(n) · Space: O(n)
Variants
Method 1 — One-pass HashMap · O(n) / O(n) ✓
Check complement before inserting. Handles [3,3] edge case correctly.
Method 2 — Two-pass HashMap · O(n) / O(n)
Build full map, then scan for complement. Slightly simpler, same complexity.
Method 3 — Two Pointers (if sorted) · O(n log n) / O(1)
Sort + two pointers. O(1) space but loses original indices.
Takeaway
Pattern: Complement Lookup via HashMap — Store visited elements, check complement on each step. Canonical HashMap-in-arrays pattern. Generalizes to 3Sum (sort + fix + two-ptr) and 4Sum.',
