SELECT '0251b8d0-9455-77ed-ba87-16410a27c71a', '521605de-ee63-77e6-9ff1-8866f0a2dbc7', 'Contains Duplicate', 'Contains Duplicate', 'Difficulty: Easy · LeetCode 217
Problem
Given an integer array nums, return true if any value appears at least twice, and false if every element is distinct.
Examples
Input
nums: int[]
1 ≤ n ≤ 105, -109 ≤ nums[i] ≤ 109
Output
boolean — true if duplicate exists
Example 1
nums = [1,2,3,1]
Output: true
// 1 appears at index 0 and 3
Example 2
nums = [1,2,3,4]
Output: false
Constraints
1 ≤ nums.length ≤ 105
-109 ≤ nums[i] ≤ 109
Understanding the problem
Brute force compares every pair — O(n²). Key insight: we only need to know "have I seen this before?" → Hash Set gives O(1) lookup, making one linear pass sufficient.
Intuition
"Have I seen this before?" → Hash Set. Store each number as we go; check the set before inserting. O(1) average lookup replaces O(n) inner scan.
Approach
Initialize empty set seen.
For each num: if num in seen → return True.
Else add num to seen.
End of loop → return False.
Naive Solution — O(n²) Brute Force
Check every pair (i, j) with i < j. Return true on first match.
Expected Output
[1,2,3,1] → true | [1,2,3,4] → false
class Solution:
    def containsDuplicate(self, nums: list[int]) -> bool:
        for i in range(len(nums)):
            for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):
                if nums[i] == nums[j]:
                    return True
        return False
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); ++i)
            for (int j = i+1; j < nums.size(); ++j)
                if (nums[i] == nums[j]) return true;
        return false;
    }
};
class Solution {
    public boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; ++i)
            for (int j = i+1; j < nums.length; ++j)
                if (nums[i] == nums[j]) return true;
        return false;
    }
}
var containsDuplicate = function(nums) {
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++)
        for (let j = i+1; j < nums.length; j++)
            if (nums[i] === nums[j]) return true;
    return false;
};
Time: O(n²) · Space: O(1)
Optimized Code — Hash Set O(n)
Expected Output
[1,2,3,1] → true | [1,2,3,4] → false | [1,1,1,3] → true
class Solution:
    def containsDuplicate(self, nums: list[int]) -> bool:
        seen = set()
        for num in nums:
            if num in seen:
                return True
            seen.add(num)
        return False
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> seen;
        for (int num : nums) {
            if (seen.count(num)) return true;
            seen.insert(num);
        }
        return false;
    }
};
class Solution {
    public boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
        for (int num : nums)
            if (!seen.add(num)) return true;
        return false;
    }
}
var containsDuplicate = function(nums) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (const num of nums) {
        if (seen.has(num)) return true;
        seen.add(num);
    }
    return false;
};
Time: O(n) · Space: O(n)
Variants
Method 1 — Hash Set · O(n) / O(n) ✓ Recommended
Build a hash set on-the-fly. O(1) lookup with early exit.
Method 2 — Sorting · O(n log n) / O(1)
Sort in-place, check adjacent elements. Better space, worse time.
Method 3 — Python One-liner · O(n) / O(n)
return len(nums) != len(set(nums)) — No early exit though.
Takeaway
Pattern: Frequency Hashing — "Have I seen this before?" → Hash Set. This is the #1 most common array pattern, foundational to Two Sum, Group Anagrams, Longest Consecutive Sequence, and hundreds more problems.',
