SELECT '4f808d09-798c-7ec2-8e90-9316e547e3cc', '6053cfde-89d0-7b63-b9d2-2655fd5b19be', 'Find the Duplicate Number', 'Find the Duplicate Number', 'Difficulty: Medium · LeetCode 287
Problem
Given an array of integers nums containing n + 1 integers where each integer is in range [1, n], find the duplicate number without modifying array in O(1) space.
Understanding the problem
Map array indices to values as an implicit linked list: index -> nums[index].
Intuition
Treat array as implicit linked list. The duplicate number represents the entrance to a cycle. Apply Floyd''s Cycle Detection in 2 phases.
Approach
Phase 1: slow = nums[0], fast = nums[0]. Move slow = nums[slow] and fast = nums[nums[fast]] until they meet.
Phase 2: Reset slow = nums[0]. Advance both pointers 1 step at a time until they meet at cycle entry point.
Return entry point value.
Naive Solution — Brute Force
Use a hash set to track seen numbers.
class Solution:
    def findDuplicate(self, nums):
        seen = set()

        for num in nums:
            if num in seen:
                return num

            seen.add(num)

        return -1
class Solution {
public:
    int findDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> seen;
        for (auto num : nums) {
            if (seen.count(num)) {
                return num;
            }
            seen.insert(num);
        }
        return -1;
    }
};
class Solution {
    public int findDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
        for (int num : nums) {
            if (seen.contains(num)) {
                return num;
            }
            seen.add(num);
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
var findDuplicate = function(nums) {
    const seen = new Set();

    for (const num of nums) {
        if (seen.has(num)) {
            return num;
        }

        seen.add(num);
    }

    return -1;
};
Time: O(n) · Space: O(n)
Optimized Code (Python 3)
class Solution:
    def findDuplicate(self, nums):
        slow = nums[0]
        fast = nums[0]

        while True:
            slow = nums[slow]
            fast = nums[nums[fast]]

            if slow == fast:
                break

        slow = nums[0]

        while slow != fast:
            slow = nums[slow]
            fast = nums[fast]

        return slow
